[{:seon.task/name "a-sweep-deletes-as-steps-of-roots-writer"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps, with runtime (root's order 2026-11-16: after the store half). What it replaces: datahike.gc-guard's reuse state (Datahike 235a8c4d), one atom changed by swaps that orders a commit's reuse of a stored value against a sweep's batch deletes, where a reuse that meets its key being deleted waits on that batch's promise. Correct, but state and a wait outside Flow (AGENTS §5). The shape: the sweep's batch deletes run as steps of root's writer proc, between its commits, so a reuse (inside a commit) and a delete never overlap: nothing to order, nothing to wait on. Datahike gives gc-storage a step form (the mark, then one batch a call, sparing the keys the commits since the mark reused, which the writer passes as a value); runtime's :collect drives it as writer steps. Holds under the rule that only root's writer commits while a sweep runs (agents' own writers are other processes: docs/storage.md#collection). DELETES: the reuse atom, its promises, konserve's :konserve.gc/admit if nothing else uses it. PRIOR WORK: datahike.gc/gc-storage!, konserve.gc/sweep!, seon.writer's :collect (runtime's), datahike.test.gc-reuse-test (its proof stays). COST: estimate per batch one writer step (one LMDB multi-dissoc, ~1k keys); per reuse nothing (a set the writer holds); the sweep's wall stretches by the commits between batches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.gc-reuse-test/a-value-reused-during-a-sweep-survives-it}
  :seon.task/estimate #:datahike.gc{gc-storage! {:n 1000, :order :linear, :ticks 100000, :bytes 100000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.system/the-store-half-of-an-update-that-loses-nothing"}}]
